  "body": "Given this case:\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n    \"pattern\": [{ \"pathname\": \"/foo/bar\" }],\r\n    \"inputs\": [{ \"pathname\": \"/foo/bar\" }],\r\n    \"expected_match\": {\r\n       \"pathname\": { \"input\": \"/foo/bar\", \"groups\": {} }\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen `process pathname for init` is called from `process a URLPatternInit`, the \"protocol\" passed to `process pathname for init` is the empty string (`\"\"`). The spec says to use the \"can-not-be-a-base\" path parsing for this, as the scheme is not special. Really this should be using the \"standard\" parsing (see [Chromium implementation](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/modules/url_pattern/url_pattern_canon.cc;l=361-362;drc=140dd96afd8d4e58d70e89e3cc4a128ec3dd1a54)). The spec should say:\r\n\r\n> If protocolValue is an empty string, or if protocolValue is a special scheme, then return the result of running canonicalize a pathname given pathnameValue.",
